## The prompt keeps growing

<At levels="simple">

Every message and every reply is added to the prompt, and the model re-reads all of it on every
call. But a model can only read so much at once: its <GlossaryPopover term="context-window">context window</GlossaryPopover>.
Long prompts are also slower and cost more. So the harness has to choose what the model sees.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Every message and every reply is added to the prompt, and the model re-reads all of it on every
call (<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" />). But a model can only read so much at once: its <GlossaryPopover term="context-window">context window</GlossaryPopover> (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />). Long before that
limit, long prompts also get slower and more expensive. So the harness has to choose what the model
sees.

</At>

Below is a long chat with the model from <ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" />. You shared six nursery rhymes, each loved
by a different child in your class, and asked what happens in each. Then you ask about the first
one. To make the problem visible we set a small budget of 600 tokens; the model itself can read far
more. Pick what the harness does when the conversation doesn't fit:

<ContextBudget />

## Three ways to make room

<At levels="simple">

- **Drop the oldest.** Simple, but here the first rhyme was the one you asked about. Without it,
  the model couldn't say which child loves it.
- **Summarize.** The harness asks the model to shrink old turns into a short note. The note kept
  "Lily", so the answer was right, but summaries can lose details that turn out to matter.
- **Retrieve.** The harness stores old turns outside the prompt and puts back the ones closest in
  meaning to the question.<Cite id="lewis2020" /> The cat rhyme came back, and the answer was right.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

- **Drop the oldest.** Simple, and often fine. But here the first rhyme was the one you asked about.
  With it gone, the model said the conversation didn't mention it. Here it admitted the gap; a model
  can just as easily fill one with a confident guess (<ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />).
- **Summarize.** The harness asks the model to condense the old turns into a short note, then keeps
  the note instead. The summary kept "Lily", so the answer was right. Summaries lose detail, though,
  and you can't always tell in advance which details will matter.
- **Retrieve.** The harness keeps the old turns stored outside the prompt, and when a question
  comes in, finds the ones most similar in meaning, using embeddings like the map in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />, and puts
  them back.<Cite id="lewis2020" /> The cat rhyme was the closest match to the question, so it came
  back and the model answered correctly.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Picking by similarity

The harness turned the question and each stored exchange into 384 numbers with MiniLM, the
embedding model from <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />, and scored each exchange by the cosine of the angle between its
vector $v$ and the question's vector $u$:

$$\cos\theta = \frac{u \cdot v}{\lVert u \rVert \, \lVert v \rVert}$$

The cat rhyme scored 0.49 and the other five 0.35 to 0.45, so it came first. None scored very low,
because every exchange shares "nursery rhyme" and "loves": close in meaning isn't always the same as
useful. The summary was cheaper still, shrinking the 149-token first exchange to a 30-token note, but
it only worked because the note happened to keep the name you later asked about. It also kept a
mistake: the model's first reply said a cat catches a mouse, which the rhyme never says, and the
summary repeated it.

</At>

This is <GlossaryPopover term="retrieval">retrieval</GlossaryPopover>, the same idea behind
assistants that search your documents: they don't read everything, they look up what seems
relevant and copy it into the prompt. Memory features work the same way: notes saved outside the
model and put back into the prompt later.

## Words or meaning

<At levels="simple">

Retrieval above found the cat rhyme by meaning. Search engines have long worked another way: a <GlossaryPopover term="keyword-search">keyword search</GlossaryPopover>
looks for the question's own words. Each finds things the other misses. Try a few questions about
the same chat:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Retrieval above found the cat rhyme by meaning. Search engines have long worked another way: a <GlossaryPopover term="keyword-search">keyword search</GlossaryPopover>
scores each text by the
question's own words it contains, after dropping common ones like "the" and "which". The usual
method, BM25, gives more credit for words that are rare among all the texts and for using them
more often, and less for long texts.<Cite id="robertson2009" /> It is exact: it finds a name, or
a code like an error number, that an embedding blurs. Anthropic's example is a search for "Error
code TS-999", where embeddings may find pages about error codes in general and miss the one about
TS-999.<Cite id="anthropic-contextual-retrieval" /> But it knows nothing of meaning: "kitty"
doesn't match "cat". Try a few questions about the same chat:

</At>

<SearchCompare />

<At levels="simple">

Words found Sam, Leo, and the crown, which meaning missed, but found nothing for "kitty", which
meaning got right. So many systems run both searches and combine the two lists, giving each text
points for how high it comes in each.<Cite id="cormack2009" /> Here that picked the right exchange
for 4 of the 5 questions; words alone got 4 and meaning alone 2.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Words found Sam, Leo, and the crown, which meaning missed: names carry little meaning, and the
crown pulled meaning toward Humpty Dumpty and the king's men. Meaning found "kitty", a word in no
exchange. So many systems run both searches and merge the two lists by rank: each text gets points
for how high it comes in each list, so one near the top of either does well.<Cite id="cormack2009" />
Anthropic found that adding keyword search to embeddings missed fewer of the right
passages.<Cite id="anthropic-contextual-retrieval" /> Here the merged list put the right exchange
first for 4 of the 5 questions, words alone for 4, and meaning alone for 2. It still missed Leo:
meaning ranked the star rhyme first and words second, which beat Leo's rhyme, first by words but
only third by meaning.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Scoring words and ranks

For a question with words $q$, BM25 scores a text $D$ as

$$
\sum_{q} \text{idf}(q)\,\frac{f(q, D)\,(k_1 + 1)}{f(q, D) + k_1\left(1 - b + b\,\frac{|D|}{\text{avgdl}}\right)},
$$

where $f(q, D)$ counts the word in the text, $|D|$ is the text's length in words, avgdl the
average length, and $k_1 = 1.2$ and $b = 0.75$ are the usual settings.<Cite id="robertson2009" />
The count saturates: a word used five times scores less than five times one use. The weight
$\text{idf}(q) = \ln\!\left(1 + \frac{N - n + 0.5}{n + 0.5}\right)$, for a word in $n$ of the $N$
texts, is highest for words in only one; the 1 inside the logarithm keeps a word found in every
text from counting against a match.

Reciprocal rank fusion merges the lists using only ranks, so it needn't compare BM25 scores with
cosines: each text scores $\sum 1/(k + r)$ over the lists that rank it, at rank $r$, with
$k = 60$.<Cite id="cormack2009" /> For Leo's question the star rhyme gets
$1/62 + 1/61 \approx 0.0325$ and Leo's rhyme $1/61 + 1/63 \approx 0.0323$.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- The model only knows what's in its prompt, and the prompt has a limit.
- When it fills up, the harness drops, summarizes, or retrieves text.
- Whatever is left out is simply gone for the model: it may say it doesn't know, or fill the gap
  with a guess.
- Keyword search finds exact words such as names; search by meaning finds the same idea in other
  words. Many systems run both and merge the results.

<At levels="deep">

- Retrieval ranks stored text by the cosine similarity of embeddings; close in meaning isn't
  always useful.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** every call re-sends the whole conversation, and the model reads all of it again.
Does it really redo all that work every time?
